<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 1235–1460 (from Sundiata Keita to the loss of the northern provinces)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Mansa to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="Djinguereber Mosque"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a><span class="soc-credit">KaTeznik · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a> around 1235, ranked people above all by birth into a lineage. Most were horon, the freeborn: the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a> line, the warrior clans who carried the quiver and led the army, Muslim clerical families, merchants and the farmers who made up the great majority. Beside them stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyamakala">nyamakala</a>, hereditary groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, smiths and leatherworkers who married among themselves and were believed to handle a dangerous force, and at the bottom the jon, the enslaved. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> ruled a loose federation of provinces and tributary kingdoms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, strong at court and in the trading towns, lived beside older beliefs in the countryside. What we know comes from Arabic writers, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in 1352–53, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, and from the oral traditions of the griots, written down centuries later and partly legendary.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 1235–1460 (from Sundiata Keita to the loss of the northern provinces)</span><span><b>People</b> c. 5–20 million (est.)</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 7</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Mansa</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Tontajon</span> <span class="soc-en">(Noble clans and governors)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–4% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Mori</span> <span class="soc-en">(Muslim scholars and judges)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Wangara</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:70%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Horon</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free farmers, fishers and herders)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 70% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:8%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Nyamakala</span> <span class="soc-en">(Griots, smiths and other craft groups)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 5–10% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:18%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Jon</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 10–25% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mansa-musa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Mansa</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Mansa Musa of Mali gave away so much gold on his way to Mecca that gold lost value in Cairo.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> was the supreme ruler, heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a>, who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumanguru-kante">Sumanguru Kanté</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a> around 1235 and was proclaimed mansa over twelve allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> kingdoms. He received tribute from provincial governors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kings, appointed governors to conquered lands, heard cases himself in solemn audiences, and kept every gold nugget found in the empire for himself, leaving only gold dust to trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sulayman">Mansa Sulayman</a>’s court in 1352–53, saw subjects answered by the mansa throw dust over their heads and backs. The mansa had to be both a pious Muslim sultan and the heir of hunter-kings protected by spirits. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1324–25, spending so much gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> that its value fell, and built mosques and palaces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>. No fixed rule settled the succession: sons, brothers and uncles disputed the throne, and in 1285 a general named Sakura, who may once have been a royal slave, seized it.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The throne passed within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a>’s lineage, to a son, brother or uncle of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>, as struggles among the royal kin and the great men of the court decided; Sakura alone broke the line, and after his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a> returned.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the royal family of the Keita lineage.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sulayman">Mansa Sulayman</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sakura">Mansa Sakura</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musas-pilgrimage">Mansa Musa's pilgrimage</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mali-empire-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 1–4% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Tontajon</span> <span class="soc-en">(Noble clans and governors)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">farin, farba</p><p class="soc-kid">Mali’s nobles fought on horseback, led the army and governed provinces for the mansa.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> stood the great lineages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> heartland. Oral tradition holds that the kings of the twelve allied lands who submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> kept their kingdoms as his farba, “great commanders”, and that sixteen clans of “quiver-bearers” formed the warrior elite; in the 14th century two of their chiefs commanded the northern and southern armies. Conquered or vital provinces were ruled by a farin, a military governor chosen by the mansa, who kept the garrison, collected taxes and customs and oversaw justice; the post was prestigious and could pass to a son with the mansa’s approval. The nobles fought as cavalry, with swords, lances, iron helmets and mail, at the head of an army that contemporary writers put at 100,000 men, 10,000 of them horsemen, raised by quotas from the free clans.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Rank came by birth into a noble lineage, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> could raise a commander to a governorship or replace a farin quickly, and a successful general, like Sakura, could reach the throne itself.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the noble warrior lineages, the clans of the quiver-bearers, and the provincial governors, with their households.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farba">Farba</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A horseman, terracotta from the Inland Niger Delta, 13th–15th century · Franko Khoury · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djenne_Terracotta_Equestrian_(13th-15th_cent).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/sankore-madrasah" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sankore-madrasah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Mori</span> <span class="soc-en">(Muslim scholars and judges)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">ulama, qadi</p><p class="soc-kid">In Mali’s trading cities, such as Timbuktu, Muslim scholars taught students the Quran and Islamic law.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> by the 10th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansas</a>, their courts and the trading towns professed it, while most villagers kept the older religion. The Muslim learned, many from clerical families, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, scribes and teachers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>, judges of Islamic law, in the towns and at court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> brought scholars back from his pilgrimage, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> jurists and the Andalusi poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-al-sahili">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</a>; the great mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, the Djinguereber, was built under him in 1327, and he sent West African scholars to study at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. Timbuktu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> grew into centres of learning, and before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> lost Timbuktu in 1433 a jurist from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> found the city full of West African scholars who knew more law than he did. Some clerical towns, such as Dia, were left to govern themselves by Islamic law alone under the Jakhanke, followers of the quietist teaching of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> Salim Suwari.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Learning ran in clerical families, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansas</a> sponsored scholarship, sending students to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and welcoming foreign scholars at court and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the scholars, judges and teachers of the towns, among them the Tarikh al-Sudan’s crowds of students in early 15th-century Timbuktu.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-al-sahili">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankore-madrasah">Sankore Madrasah</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mali-empire-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Wangara</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">juula</p><p class="soc-kid">Mali’s merchants traded gold for salt, which camels carried in great slabs across the Sahara Desert.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Trade made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> rich. Mande-speaking Muslim merchants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Wangara</a> or Dyula, carried gold from the fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bure">Bure</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a> from the forest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, and brought back salt cut by slaves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a> in the desert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from Takedda; they met North African traders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walata">Walata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, where the caravans crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> arrived. By the early 14th century Mali supplied almost half the gold of the Old World. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> taxed every load of gold, copper and salt that crossed his borders, and payments were made in gold dust, salt, copper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> praised the security of the roads, on which neither traveller nor resident had anything to fear. Merchants travelled with slaves who carried their goods, and slaves were themselves traded: the caravan Ibn Battuta joined for home in 1353 took 600 enslaved women north. Merchant families also spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> along the trade routes, far into the forest zone.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Trade ran in Muslim merchant families who settled along the routes; wealth bought slaves and horses, but rank still came from birth into a lineage.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the Wangara and other merchant families of the trading towns and routes.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Wangara</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-saharan-trade">Trans-Saharan trade</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">Gold trade</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Market day at Djenné, Mali (photograph, 2006): the Wangara traded gold, kola nuts and cloth for Saharan salt and copper · Ferdinand Reus · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djenne_market.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mali-empire-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 70% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Horon</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free farmers, fishers and herders)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people in Mali were farmers who grew millet and cotton and lived in villages with their relatives.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>’s people were free farmers, members of horon lineages living in villages of related households. They grew grain, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, working the land with iron tools made by the smiths, and raised sheep, goats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-niger-delta">inland delta</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> the Bozo, Somono and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorko">Sorko</a> lived by fishing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> herders kept cattle. The heads of the families in each village chose a village head from the line of its founder, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>’s authority reached them mainly through tribute and the provincial governor. Free men owed military service: each clan sent a quota of fighting men, who came with their own weapons, bows and poisoned arrows in the south and spears and hide shields in the north. Visitors described a fertile, crowded land, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a>, written in the 17th century, claimed that the villages around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> stood so close that a message could be shouted from one to the next.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Free status came by birth into a horon lineage; capture in war or in a raid could turn free people into slaves.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: the free farming, fishing and herding families, the great majority. The other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Bozo fishermen on the Niger, a people who have fished the inland delta for centuries (photograph, 2006) · Ferdinand Reuss · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bozo_Fishermen.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/griots" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/griots.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 5–10% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Nyamakala</span> <span class="soc-en">(Griots, smiths and other craft groups)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">jeli, numu, garanke</p><p class="soc-kid">Griots were musicians and storytellers who remembered the history of Mali’s kings and sang it aloud.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Beside the freeborn stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyamakala">nyamakala</a>, hereditary groups who practised special crafts and married only among themselves: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a> (jeli), musicians, praise-singers and keepers of genealogies; the smiths (numu), who made the tools and weapons of an empire rich in iron; and the leatherworkers (garanke). Their name links them to nyama, a vital force, dangerous as well as powerful, believed to flow through their work, so they were both needed and feared. At court the griot was tutor to the princes, master of ceremonies and adviser to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>, and in Mande memory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a>’s own griot, Balla Fasséké, founded the Kouyaté line of griots that still exists. Every noble family had its griots, who could praise or shame the mighty in public. Historians debate how far these groups already formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> in the 14th century: Tal Tamari dates the rise of castes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> to about the 13th century, Susan McIntosh to about the 10th. Later Mande society ranked the nyamakala below the freeborn.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Membership came by birth and marriage stayed within the group; a griot rose by the favour of a great patron, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>’s griot was among the most influential men at court.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: in Mande societies the hereditary craft groups of griots, smiths, leatherworkers and weavers made up a substantial minority.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">Griots</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyamakala">Nyamakala</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mali-empire-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 10–25% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Jon</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people in Mali were owned by others and made to work without pay, and some were sold across the Sahara.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The enslaved, jon in Mande, were captives taken in war and in raids on neighbouring peoples, the children of enslaved mothers, and people bought in the markets. They worked the fields of the great households, carried merchants’ goods, and served at court and in the army, and in the desert at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a> the salt was dug by slaves. Some were sold north across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>: the caravan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> joined in 1353 carried 600 enslaved women to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and later Arabic accounts say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> took some 12,000 slaves on his pilgrimage, a figure historians treat with caution; Michael Gomez estimates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> may have captured over 6,000 people a year to prepare for it. Ibn Battuta noted that Malians vied with one another in the number of slaves they owned. Slave soldiers called sofa, armed by the state, served in the garrisons under their own commanders, and slaves of the royal household could become powerful. Oral tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a>’s charter with a rule against ill-treating slaves.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom could come by an owner’s grant, and slaves of the court and the army could win wealth and power, as Sakura, perhaps a freed slave of the royal family, did; later Mande custom set people born into a household apart from those bought or captured.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: Ibn Battuta noted that Malians vied with one another in the number of slaves they owned, and slaves farmed for the king and the nobles; later Sahelian states often held a fifth to a third of their people in slavery.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Slabs of Saharan rock salt for sale at Mopti (photograph, 2003): in Mali's time the salt of Taghaza was dug by slaves · Robin Taylor · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salt_selling_Mopti_Mali.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="mali-empire">Be born in the Mali Empire</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Rank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> came chiefly by birth: a person belonged to a lineage, free, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyamakala">nyamakala</a> or enslaved, and married within it. Service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> could still carry men far. Sakura, a general who may once have been a slave of the royal family, seized the throne in 1285 and led Mali to new conquests, and the mansa could make or unmake provincial governors at will. War moved people down the ladder: raids and conquests turned free people of neighbouring lands into captives, and the trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> carried some of them away. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> offered another path. Learning and the pilgrimage brought prestige, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> sent West Africans to study at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> found a place at court and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sulayman">Mansa Sulayman</a>’s death around 1360, a string of short reigns and palace coups showed how much power great officials could seize, and an officer called the kankoro-sigui became a power behind the throne.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A woman took her place from her lineage, free, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyamakala">nyamakala</a> or enslaved, and married within it. Royal women could hold real power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sulayman">Mansa Sulayman</a> ruled with his principal wife and cousin Qasa as co-ruler until, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> in 1352–53, he imprisoned her on a charge of plotting with an exiled prince and raised another wife, Banju, who was not of royal blood; Qasa took refuge with the preacher of the mosque, and her fate is not recorded. Mande tradition honours mothers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a>’s name means “Sogolon’s lion”, after the mother whom the epic makes central to his story. Ibn Battuta, a strict Muslim jurist, was shocked that Malian women, from slave girls to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>’s daughters, did not cover themselves as he expected. Enslaved women had the least protection: the 600 women in the caravan he joined for home were bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as servants and concubines.</p></section>
