e-5.jpg","you":"You farm millet and cotton with your kin in a village run by its family heads, send your sons with their own bows when your clan’s quota is called, and pay tribute to the mansa’s governor.","kid":"Most people in Mali were farmers who grew millet and cotton and lived in villages with their relatives.","rise":"Free status came by birth into a horon lineage; capture in war or in a raid could turn free people into slaves.","why":"Most of Mali’s people were free farmers, members of horon lineages living in villages of related households.","people":[]},{"n":6,"lv":5,"band":"specialists","name":"Griots, smiths and other craft groups","loc":"Nyamakala","term":"jeli, numu, garanke","lbl":"c. 5–10% (est.)","w":7.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/griots.jpg","you":"You were born into a family of griots and sing the genealogies of the noble family you serve, whose name your words can raise or shame.","kid":"Griots were musicians and storytellers who remembered the history of Mali’s kings and sang it aloud.","rise":"Membership came by birth and marriage stayed within the group; a griot rose by the favour of a great patron, and the mansa’s griot was among the most influential men at court.","why":"Beside the freeborn stood the nyamakala, hereditary groups who practised special crafts and married only among themselves: the griots (jeli), musicians, praise-singers and keepers of genealogies; the smiths (numu), who…","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":6,"band":"enslaved","name":"Enslaved people","loc":"Jon","term":"","lbl":"c. 10–25% (est.)","w":17.5,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/mali-empire-7.jpg","you":"You were captured in a raid and now work your owner’s fields or carry his goods to market; sent to the army instead, you would serve the mansa as a soldier in a garrison.","kid":"Enslaved people in Mali were owned by others and made to work without pay, and some were sold across the Sahara.","rise":"Freedom could come by an owner’s grant, and slaves of the court and the army could win wealth and power, as Sakura, perhaps a freed slave of the royal family, did; later Mande custom set people born into a household apart from those bought or captured.","why":"The enslaved, jon in Mande, were captives taken in war and in raids on neighbouring peoples, the children of enslaved mothers, and people bought in the markets.","people":[]}]}</script>
